Ported (Python to Rust) into crates/kgraf-orchestrator/src/guards.rs:
`DelegationPolicy` (allowed_targets table with "*", max_depth 3 in 1..=8,
max_message_chars 8000 in 1..=100000, range checks raising on bad values) and
the six guards of `AgentDelegateTool._delegate`, in upstream's order: task
present and within the length limit; target on the roster; delegation trace
ends with the source; target already in the trace is a cycle; the policy
table allows the pair; depth within max_depth. The `@` prefix strip on the
target is kept.

Deliberately different, and why:
- The trace is the contract lineage and the roster is the set of configured
  routes. Upstream's agents are standing identities; kGraf's delegating
  unit is a contract, whose handed-back remainder usually continues on the
  same route, so "same agent again" is normal. A cycle is therefore a child
  task that normalizes (whitespace, case) to an ancestor's task.
- The policy default allows every configured pair; upstream's default
  forbids source == target, for the reason above.
- Upstream's `timeout_seconds` and relay locks are not ported: a worker is
  bounded by its step budget and the admission limits, and each child runs in
  sequence in its parent's worktree.
